Native daemon-runtime maturity gate
===================================
Build the static VM-only PID 1 fixture with:
  make -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu) -C /usr/src/tools/test/daemon-runtime all
Build libservice, BSDFilesystem and their tests from the same source state first.

run-vm.py requires --root (a NO_ROOT staged world), --objtop (the standard
/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64), a new --work beneath /usr/obj, --qemu and
--qemu-data. It requires exact staged/object kernel hashes and matching kernel
configurations, and static test/fixture binaries. It copies inputs into its
own staging tree, with root ownership represented in a makefs METALOG.
No host root login, install, mount or reboot is needed. It does not write the
input staging root or attach another VM's writable disk. Compilation uses all
logical CPUs; this small runtime guest uses four CPUs and 1 GiB RAM by default.
--cpus selects one through four guest CPUs in the default fixture profile.
An explicit --resource-profile bazel-builder permits up to 8 vCPUs and 24576
MiB guest RAM; callers must still select --cpus, --memory-mib and the build
host RSS budget. Default fixture memory/CPU bounds remain unchanged.

The VM has no network. By default it boots a PID 1 fixture with the capability
plane disabled; --oes-admission enables the additional integration described below.
It runs native session/bootstrap deadline and session lifecycle tests as root,
plus the BSDFilesystem descriptor-hardening failure regression.
A skip, missing/duplicate pass marker, timeout, panic, WITNESS reversal or
unclean shutdown fails the run. inputs.json and result.json retain input hashes
and the exact QEMU command; serial.log retains output, including failed runs.
result.json also records vm_elapsed_seconds and a serial_timeline of complete
construction, case, cleanup and VM markers. Times are host-observed seconds since
launch and include the one-second poll interval and serial buffering. They are
diagnostic observations, not guest execution durations or an acceptance oracle.
Repeated markers remain visible; partial lines are held until complete. These
records do not change case timeouts or the existing pass/failure checks.
The default gate does not qualify booted daemons, actual OOM, ACPI suspend, hardware,
installer behavior, or a complete system image. Never run runtime_init as the
host's init. Source and object inputs must be coordinated before full acceptance.

Use --client-ownership to include the static audit, crypto, trace and log
client lifecycle suites. Build these libraries/tests first. The runner records
each binary hash and derives the required cases from its ATF listing; each
case gets its own directory and declared timeout (40 seconds if omitted). No skip is a pass.
These suites exercise real descriptor operations with service test doubles;
they do not replace integrated daemon/provider tests.

Use --oes-factory to stage the current object tree's oes.ko and boot it in the
disposable guest. The runner hashes the module and static factory and handoff tests.
This exercises real OES NOTIFY descriptor attenuation after cap_enter, including
denial of AUTH subscriptions and mode/scope changes.
The handoff suite also drops the recipient to UID/GID65534 with no
supplementary groups before requesting and using a client; the factory stays
privileged. Child assertions are fatal and propagated to the root ATF parent.
After the factory session closes, both recipients use liboes to adopt the fd,
query mode/scope, subscribe and unsubscribe NOTIFY events, read subscription
sets and statistics, reject a scope change, and destroy the owned descriptor.
This tests descriptor delivery to a non-root recipient, not .ucl admission. It does not validate
SwitchBoard attribute admission. The handoff cases exercise real channel
descriptor delivery, denied onward transfer, malformed-request rejection, and
the five-second idle deadline.

Use --oes-admission for the additional real Capsule/SwitchBoard .ucl allow/deny
stack. Build its private static fixtures with tools/test/oes-admission/build.py;
see that directory for the exact scope and invocation. This option implies
--oes-factory and uses an OES-only provider with production factory code.

The OES gate also checks factory quota boundaries and ordinary filesystem
mediation of a real intermediate-directory alias to /dev/oes. It proves the
alias resolves before mediation, is denied by the grant path, and an ordinary
/dev/null grant remains usable. The shipped manifest parser test is separate;
neither check substitutes for full SwitchBoard client admission.

Use --log-store to run every static BSDLog store_test case, including partial
write rollback, failed rollback admission, reopening recovery, privacy, scoped
queries, reclamation and retention. Tests use real files with injected write
and truncation errors; they do not qualify physical power-loss durability.

Use --log-storage to run the storage_test manager suite and storage_barrier_test.
The manager suite exercises actual processes, sockets, shared rings, concurrent
writers, backpressure, identity scoping, reclamation and restart. It uses the
manager test entry point without activating its production sandbox. The barrier
case exercises the real packet handler with injected ring/storage results; it
verifies terminal flush/query ingestion errors, healthy peers and retryable
sync-only errors. It is not a full-provider disk-exhaustion acceptance test.

Use --log-provider for provider dispatch and ring lifecycle tests over real
capability channels. The sink is injected; cases cover backend errors, trusted
receive timestamps, malformed descriptor rejection and terminal ring-drain
failures during flush/detach. This does not activate a full logging bundle.

Additional static ATF programs can be selected with repeated --atf-suite paths
relative to --objtop, for example:
    --atf-suite usr.sbin/BSDPower/tests/provider_test
    --atf-suite lib/libnotify/tests/watch_test
Only self-contained tests are suitable: this fixture supplies neither installed
suite data nor a full service plane. Paths must remain inside objtop. The runner
records the source, private alias, enumerated cases and stable input hash in its
provenance. Every selected case must pass; skips and missing results fail the
run. Each case uses its declared timeout, or 40 seconds if omitted. This does not replace full
bundle activation, authority admission, production sandbox or hardware tests.

The BSDPower reactor_test runs the production sessions_run loop with three
real capability channels. Listener identity and quiesce controls are injected.
It verifies two simultaneous inhibitors, per-owner disconnect cleanup, suspend
blocking, idle quiesce refusal, and explicit stop cleanup while the server
process remains alive. It never opens /dev/acpi. SwitchBoard admission and
actual hardware transitions still need full-system acceptance.

The reactor suite also fills four sessions for one injected label and 64 across
distinct labels, rejects excess clients, and verifies slot/lease-owner reuse
after disconnect. Its lifecycle case waits through the real 30-second idle
limit: an unleased session expires while a lease holder remains usable. Allow
at least 40 seconds per case and enough VM wall time for boot plus that interval.

Use --atf-case relative/path:case (repeatable) to run a specific listed ATF
case from a static object-tree binary. Unknown case names are rejected. The
same path, static-link and hash checks apply as for --atf-suite. inputs.json
and result.json record the exact selected cases; a selected-case pass does
not certify the rest of that suite. The guest configures lo0 with 127.0.0.1/8 for local IPv4 TCP/UDP
fixtures. QEMU still has no network adapter or external connectivity. IPv6
loopback and deterministic unresponsive peers are not configured.

Cleanup callback versus unload regression
-----------------------------------------
Build the private fixture with:
    make -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu) -C /usr/src/tools/test/daemon-runtime/cleanup-fixture SYSDIR=/usr/src/sys
Build the selected ATF binary statically with:
    make -B -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu) -C /usr/src/tests/sys/mac_capability NO_SHARED.mac_capability_test=yes mac_capability_test
Add these options to the normal run-vm.py invocation:
    --cleanup-lifecycle
    --atf-case tests/sys/mac_capability/mac_capability_test:unload_waits_for_cleanup_callback
The runner stages and hashes the test-only module from the standard object
root. The test loads it inside the guest, blocks a revoke callback, starts
module unload, confirms unload remains blocked for one second, then releases
cleanup and checks both syscalls finish. It repeats with final close owning a
zero-reference instance, using a pipe gate to make the last close deterministic.
The kernel fixture bounds an unreleased
callback at ten seconds. It is not part of the installed base; never install
or load this fixture on the host. Without the staged module this case skips;
the VM runner treats a selected-case skip as failure. This covers the running
callback/unload ordering, not every close/refcount interleaving or async service
resource obligation. Keep WITNESS/INVARIANTS close/unload stress on the VM list.

Build fixtures against syscall headers and static libraries matching the guest
kernel. A source kernel with renumbered 5BSD syscalls cannot use syscall stubs
from an older host libc.a. A complete matching world build supplies these;
standalone test builds must explicitly link the matching libsys.a ahead of
host libc.a. Verify the linked executable, not only header macros. The runner
rejects skipped hardening checks and does not treat ENOSYS as acceptance.

The same --cleanup-lifecycle fixture supports
    --atf-case tests/sys/mac_capability/mac_capability_test:failed_init_completes_cleanup
It injects initialization failure sixteen times against a four-instance limit,
checks EIO and unchanged active
instance counts, verifies revoke was not called for failed instances, then
checks successful creation/close and module unload. Initialization rollback
must publish cleanup completion even though it deliberately skips co_revoke.

The --cleanup-lifecycle case
    --atf-case tests/sys/mac_capability/mac_capability_test:unload_waits_for_admission
blocks authorization and initialization while another process unloads the
provider. It covers direct CONNECT and MINT_INSTANCE, success and failure
returns (eight interleavings), verifies unload waits, checks rejected creation,
rejects mint on descriptors retained after unload, and checks instance counts
after rollback. This does not force every allocator
failure or replace WITNESS/INVARIANTS stress testing.

The mandatory session payload/attachment case prints SESSION_DEADLINE with
requested and elapsed milliseconds, calling-thread CPU milliseconds, and
voluntary/involuntary context switches around its timeout call. The existing
75 ms allowance for a 10 ms request is unchanged. Retain failing runs even if
a later run passes; low CPU time alone does not identify whether excess elapsed
time came from scheduling, timer delivery, lock contention, or another wait.

For deadline-overrun diagnosis, select
  --atf-case lib/libservice/tests/libservice_api_test:deadline_timer_control
This takes 128 plain 10ms nanosleeps without session/channel operations and
checks the same 75ms allowance. It is diagnostic, not a substitute for the
mandatory session test; either failure keeps the run failed. The timer control
can also run on the host without capability operations. Compare results to
distinguish a service-only failure from broader guest timing delays. Both
inputs.json and result.json include host_loadavg_start and host_logical_cpus;
these are context, not proof that host scheduling caused a failure.

2026-10-10 investigation: deadline-timer-control VM failed the service check
(411.978ms) and timer control (33/128 overruns, maximum348.478ms). The same
host timer control passed (maximum10.063ms). Concurrent host load was24.64
with builds and other QEMU processes. This demonstrates VM timing delays
independent of libservice, but does not distinguish emulator scheduling from
guest timer delivery or establish the original failure's sole cause. A quiet
host/hardware-accelerated comparison and guest timer trace remain required.
An icount experiment exited before tests with QEMU "Bad icount read"; no
virtual-clock option is shipped and no failing allowance was relaxed.

Diagnostic comparisons may select --eventtimer LAPIC/i8254/HPET and
--tcg-thread multi/single. Defaults retain automatic guest timer selection
and multi-thread TCG. The requested settings are recorded in provenance;
RUNTIME_CLOCK prints actual guest eventtimer and timecounter selections.
A timer override can fall back if unavailable, so inspect the actual value.
Both modes retain the selected guest CPU count and all acceptance thresholds.

Further 2026-10-10 host ktrace captured a QEMU CPU thread descheduled for
174.972ms while its main thread waited175.002ms on the same mutex; the CPU
thread resumed and woke the main thread. CPU deschedules reached936.947ms.
This demonstrates host lock-holder scheduling delays in the failing VM.
Both LAPIC/i8254 and single/multi TCG reproduced overruns. No production
timeout change or relaxed allowance follows from this result; repeat latency
acceptance on a quiet host and keep the timer control. Tracing can perturb
timings and does not prove the sole cause of every earlier untraced failure.

The --oes-admission option now also requires live coalition reload/removal
acceptance using a private tmpfs bundle. See tools/test/oes-admission/README.
--fixture-objtop selects a separately built static fixture tree beneath
/usr/obj/usr/src, leaving --objtop as the authoritative kernel/module tree.
This keeps fixture compilation from overwriting active world-build objects;
all staged hashes, kernel checks and pass requirements still apply.

Real ZFS provisioning and ATF cleanup
------------------------------------
--zfs adds zfs.ko from the selected kernel build and zpool, zfs, truncate and sh
from --root. ELF dependencies are resolved within that world, copied and hashed;
no host libraries or host pools are used. --memory-mib supplies the guest RAM
(default 1024); the storage qualification uses 2048. Example additions:
    --zfs --memory-mib 2048 --timeout 360
    --atf-suite usr.sbin/BSDFilesystem/tests/anchor_test
The test creates its own sparse file-backed pool inside the disposable guest.
This is a dataset-capability mechanism test, not BIP bootstrap import, reboot
persistence, package-derived generation construction or protected upgrades.

The runner reads ATF timeout and has.cleanup metadata, records it in inputs.json,
and runs declared cleanup even after body failure or timeout. Body and cleanup
use separate process groups. Both statuses and explicit cleanup markers must
succeed before the case passes. Skipped bodies remain failures. A failed cleanup
cannot be hidden by a passed body. Cleanup has the same declared time limit.

Build runner_probe alongside runtime_init to test this reporting contract.
Select cleanup_pass for a positive control. cleanup_fail, body_fail and
body_timeout are deliberate negative controls: each must fail qualification;
body_fail and body_timeout must still emit their cleanup-executed messages.
They are not product acceptance tests. metadata_test.py checks malformed ATF
metadata and rejection of missing/duplicate cleanup evidence on the host.

Explicit-device pool import
---------------------------
--scratch-disk-mib SIZE accepts 128..65536 MiB. It creates a new
private scratch.img under the new work directory, attaches it as the guest's
second virtio disk, and supplies /tests/scratch-device. It never accepts a host
disk or pool. The disk remains with the result for inspection; no later run
silently reuses it. The size and QEMU device arguments are recorded.
Without --zfs, an explicit --scratch-host-max-allocated-mib budget is required;
this mode does not stage world-built ZFS tools or modules. The caller must
supply its declared guest tools and matching execution-kernel requirements.
--scratch-host-reserve-mib sets the host free-space floor (default 1024 MiB,
minimum 256 MiB). Preflight requires free space for budget plus reserve.
The monitor samples private-disk allocation and host free space, checks file
identity/logical capacity and terminates QEMU on a breached bound. Results
retain samples and observed maxima/minima; these are not complete I/O accounting,
inter-sample peak guarantees or controlled performance measurements. Existing
--zfs callers retain their bootstrap tool/module behavior and may opt into an
allocation budget. Filesystems are created and mounted only inside the guest.
Example additions for the production import primitive and recipient checks:
    --zfs --memory-mib 2048 --scratch-disk-mib 1024 --timeout 360
    --atf-suite usr.sbin/capsule/tests/storage_test
    --atf-suite usr.sbin/capsule/tests/import_test
    --atf-suite usr.sbin/switchboard/tests/boot_storage_test
The destructive disk case requires that explicit VM role. It creates and exports
a pool, then calls the production acquisition primitive with the observed GUIDs.
It tests identity/device rejection, confined derivation and strict cleanup.
The fixture is not Capsule PID 1 and has BIP disabled; it cannot qualify the
retained-anchor bootstrap RPC or a protected persistent provider.

Explicit additional fixture data
-------------------------------
--extra-root adds a caller-supplied fixture tree after runner-owned files are
staged. Regular files, directories and relative file symlinks within the supplied
tree are accepted; absolute, escaping, dangling, cyclic and directory aliases are
rejected before copying. Replacing existing files or symlinks is refused. Common directories retain runner metadata. The
makefs image uses root ownership and preserves explicit extra-file modes; runner
files retain mode 0555 and directories retain the existing runner policy.
File hashes, modes, symlink targets and the source root are retained in
inputs.json/result.json. METALOG paths and link targets use octal escaping where
needed, including the installed [ alias.
An explicit etc/passwd prevents creation of the fallback passwd stub; it does not
permit replacement of other runner-owned inputs. Generated account databases must
be staged with their installation modes, not copied from raw Bazel output modes. This is fixture provisioning, not
a package-derived system inventory or installed-system acceptance.

Private artifact output disk
----------------------------
--output-disk-mib creates a new mode-0600 output.img under the fresh VM work
directory. It requires a read-only --input-disk and accepts capacities 16..16384
MiB. The root disk remains first, input is /dev/vtbd1 and output is /dev/vtbd2;
/tests/output-device declares the output slot. Output attachment uses structured
QEMU block options. Existing host disks cannot be selected as the output.

After shutdown the runner records output_disk_sha256, including failed/partial
runs. A hash is content binding, not acceptance or generation authentication.
output_disk_test.py uses explicit root/object/fixture/QEMU/input-disk arguments,
rejects a missing input before staging, and runs both raw input write-refusal and
output first/last-block write-and-flush probes. Host verification checks the entire
output hash/size/permissions and unchanged input. output-disk-vm-v2 passed.
This is an export mechanism test; the output is test bytes, not a bootable OS.

Anonymous mount admission mechanism test
---------------------------------------
The anon_mount_test executable and anon-mount-fixture/mac_anon_mount_test.ko
exercise real ZFS handle mounting with a VM-only rejecting MAC policy. Never
load this fixture on the host or include it in release images. Build the module
with KERNBUILDDIR pointing to the selected matching kernel object directory;
build anon_mount_test through the ordinary buildenv and this Makefile.
Stage the module under boot/kernel in an explicit --extra-root and select
--zfs --atf-case tools/test/daemon-runtime/anon_mount_test:admission. The selected
--root supplies the matching boot kernel and ZFS userland tools; existing runner
hashing, ABI checks, case accounting, cleanup and shutdown checks apply.

The test creates its own file-backed pool, opens a ZH_MOUNT handle, then loads
the policy. A child drops to UID 65534. Sixteen rejected creations must expose
no mount or directory descriptor and must balance mount-label initialization
and destruction. Admission observes the original UID separately from ZFS's
kernel credential substitution. A permitted creation must return a usable ZFS
directory descriptor; final anchor close must balance the seventeenth label
and restore the mount count. Cleanup destroys the test pool. No pathname or
UID in this fixture defines production BIP policy. This does not test persistent
state boundaries, credential changes after admission,
service operation, Secure Boot, or recovery.

The admission test also denies access through a second handle joining a cached
mount, then permits a retry without losing the original anchor. It forces a
last-anchor unmount failure to leave an orphan, denies root-vnode open during
adoption, then permits recovery and verifies final label and mount cleanup.
Orphan lookup precedes new creation: no admission error is reinterpreted as
permission to recover an existing mount. Both cached and adopted mounts use
current vn_open_vnode policy. This verifies recovery plumbing with a synthetic
policy, not authenticated BIP provider recovery or persistent-state authority.

The same test rejects a read-only request for a writable orphan and a writable
request for an existing read-only mount. It verifies EEXIST/EROFS respectively,
unchanged creation counts on mismatch, actual MNT_RDONLY on the permitted
read-only descriptor, and final cleanup of all eighteen mount labels. A new
handle cannot change the mode of a shared mount. These checks do not establish
that arbitrary dataset replacement or reboot recovery is authenticated.

Runtime container identity regression
-------------------------------------
Use --atf-suite usr.sbin/switchboard/tests/run_container_identity_test with
an explicitly selected matching kernel/root. The three cases cover formerly
colliding labels, global/personal owner separation, complete long labels,
repeatability and truncation/empty-input refusal. They exercise the naming
helper only, not supervisor launches, symlink refusal or persistent authority.

The companion --atf-suite
usr.sbin/switchboard/tests/run_container_directory_test exercises the actual
child setup helper. It renames a held directory and replaces its old pathname,
checks that the working directory still matches the held inode, checks removal
of CAP_FCHDIR without removal of other rights, and requires missing launch
rights to fail without changing cwd. This is helper coverage, not a complete
supervisor launch or a protected-boot replacement attack.

The directory suite also covers fresh_only: successful creation, refusal of
existing nonempty/empty directories and symlinks, preservation of refused
contents and metadata, and fresh creation after removal while the old directory
handle is held. It does not exercise supervisor failure/coalition cleanup.

Use --atf-suite usr.sbin/switchboard/tests/launch_gate_test for real execute.c
launch-gate faults and abort/container rollback. The latter uses real directory
operations and a pdfork child: pre-fork abort allows retry; post-fork abort
preserves storage for the child and refuses reuse until child exit and explicit
cleanup. Authority and coalition callbacks are stubbed. This does not establish
whole-supervisor or protected-boot lifecycle acceptance.

--atf-suite usr.sbin/switchboard/tests/session_issuer_test checks the production
issuer-liveness predicate with a real pdfork child: live before and after
cap_enter, refused after confirmed exit. Invalid, closed and non-process
descriptors are refused. Liveness does not enroll an issuer or authenticate a
user. The complete broker/policy/lease path still requires protected-boot tests.

The anonymous mount admission case also writes and fsyncs an identity marker
through the real directory descriptor before orphaning the mount, then verifies
its exact contents after recovery. This proves data continuity for the tested
recovery path; it does not inject a shortened-fsid collision or qualify BIP.

Select anon_mount_test:identity_collision to exercise wrong-dataset orphan
lookup. The disposable MAC module's write-only alias_fsid control accepts a
vnode descriptor plus a replacement fsid and changes only an anonymous ZFS
mount's lookup identifier. It neither changes the objset identity nor adds a
production bypass. The case creates two datasets, aliases one mount's fsid to
the other's, orphans it, and requires ESTALE when the other handle mounts.
It verifies surviving data, restores the identifier, reopens both datasets,
and checks separate contents and balanced mount/label cleanup. Never load this
fault module on the host or stage it in production images.

Runtime helper compatibility
----------------------------
Before creating a VM disk, run-vm.py queries the selected static runtime_init
with --describe and validates its metadata version, cleanup support and maximum
case timeout. This query performs no guest initialization and is safe outside
PID 1. Missing or incompatible descriptions require rebuilding the selected
fixture; the runner does not silently substitute an object-tree binary. The
accepted contract and helper hash are retained with the run provenance.

Private writable copies of existing offline disks
-----------------------------------------------
With --input-disk/--input-disk-sha256 and --output-disk-mib, select
--output-disk-source and --output-disk-source-sha256 to initialize the private
output.img from an offline disk. The source must be a regular nonsymlink file
whose exact size matches the explicit capacity. It is read only by the host
copy helper, never attached to QEMU. The guest sees only the independent copy
as /dev/vtbd2; the separate input remains read-only at /dev/vtbd1. This allows
state-initialization tests to retain original allocation evidence.

Copy creation is exclusive and mode 0600, checks copied bytes and source hashes,
and fsyncs the copy. Existing outputs and source symlinks/FIFOs are refused.
The result records the source identity/hash and verifies the source is unchanged
after shutdown; a changed source fails the run. Output hashes describe even
failed/partial guest state, not accepted system generations. Select quiescent
sources in the exclusive trusted builder. Do not use a running VM's disk.

Run python3.12 -B disk_copy_test.py for native source-isolation and refusal
checks. These checks do not qualify guest initialization, ZFS import/export,
cold-boot persistence, normal startup or BIP enforcement.

Extra-root hard links are preserved by source device/inode identity, with the
alias-to-canonical mapping retained in the fixture receipt. Equal file bytes
alone never create a hard link. Explicitly shared runner files can anchor an
alias group; incompatible existing shared inodes or modes are rejected before
copying. This preserves staging identity for makefs, whose directory traversal
and mtree loader both recognize hard links. Guest inode verification remains
part of installed-layout qualification, not a consequence of staging tests.

Bazel ATF installed helper paths
-------------------------------
The native ATF adapter defaults to its declared runfiles bin directory. An
installed-image qualification launcher can explicitly invoke:

    /usr/tests/bazel/runner --installed-path /bin:/usr/bin

The argument supplies helper command search directories, not a shell fragment.
Every entry must be a nonempty existing absolute directory; relative entries,
empty components and non-directories are rejected before test setup. Inherited
PATH never selects this mode. ATF harness/data, candidate libraries and configured
executables still come from the declared runfiles. Accordingly this qualifies
installed helper behavior with native test artifacts, not an entirely installed
system or a production boot. The fixture must separately verify the installed
program/library inventory and dependency closure. Configured executable files
retain their original basenames under distinct atf-config/<index>/ directories.
